'''Jingū Minato''' was a Sanese mercantile colony on the eastern [[Jade Peninsula]] established during the reign of Empress [[Jingū of Miyagi]]. It was founded on a coastal district that had previously owed allegiance to the [[Qi state]], and it became one of the earliest permanent Sanese footholds on the continent. The colony was separated from the peninsula's interior by the [[Rìluò range]] and lay east of [[Jiaozhi]] and southeast of [[Haizhou]].

The colony was established in the aftermath of the [[Zongyuan-Miyagi War]], when the Sanese court sought to secure a permanent commercial and strategic position on the continent. Jingū Minato functioned both as a mercantile depot and as a buffer settlement, allowing Sanese merchants to participate in overland exchange while strengthening the court's presence along the eastern Jade Peninsula.

== Foundation ==

Jingū Minato was founded as part of the expansionist policy associated with Empress Jingū's western expedition. The colony was not a full territorial annexation in the later imperial sense, but rather a controlled mercantile settlement established in a district of uncertain political loyalty. Its position on the coast made it suitable for naval supply, storage, and exchange with continental merchants.

The Sanese presence in Jingū Minato depended on access to the sea and on the defensive barrier of the Rìluò range, which separated the colony from the inland powers of the eastern Jade Peninsula. This geography allowed the colony to operate as a commercial enclave while remaining tied to Sanese naval power.

== Role ==

Jingū Minato served three related functions. It provided the Sanese court with a continental market for maritime goods, it offered a foothold for diplomatic contact with the eastern Jade Peninsula, and it helped shield Sanese shipping from hostile inland powers. Later traditions remembered the colony as a strategic buffer as much as a place of trade.

The colony also marked an early stage in the formation of Sanese overseas policy. Rather than simply raid or trade at sea, the court attempted to hold a fixed settlement on foreign soil, suggesting a more durable conception of maritime influence.

== Loss ==

By around 100 CE, Jingū Minato was lost during the [[Zongyuan-Miyagi War]], within the broader context of the [[Eternal Zongic Wars]]. The precise circumstances of its disappearance are not fully recorded, but the colony no longer appears in the historical record after this period. Its loss ended the earliest phase of Sanese continental colonial presence on the eastern Jade Peninsula.

Later historians treated the colony's fate as part of the broader instability that reshaped the peninsula in the centuries before the rise of Great Zhongyuan. In that sense, Jingū Minato became a reminder that Sanese continental footholds could be established through naval power but remained vulnerable to inland war.

== Legacy ==

Jingū Minato occupied an important place in later Sanese historical memory as the first durable mercantile colony founded on the eastern Jade Peninsula. It was remembered both as a symbol of Jingū's expansionist authority and as an early example of the strategic settlements that would become more common in later maritime policy.

Its disappearance around 100 CE also gave the colony a limited but lasting role in the historiography of the Eternal Zongic Wars. Later writers used it to illustrate the fragility of commercial colonies placed on contested frontiers, especially where a coastal settlement depended on access through hostile or unstable inland territory.

